// THREE PAGES. The point of this fixture is the VERTICAL, which no other committed
// fixture pins: until it existed, a change to page breaking or to the page constants
// could move every gap on every page and no snapshot would notice (HANDOFF 2-8).
//
// Three is the smallest count that separates the three cases the page breaker treats
// differently:
//   page 1      -- filled by the breaker, gaps stretched to the force it solved for
//   page 2      -- a middle page, same force, but no header reserved above it
//   page 3      -- the LAST page, which ragged-last-bottom hands the force of the page
//                  BEFORE it rather than the natural distance
//                  (LILYPOND-REF: lily/page-breaking.cc:570-573 fixed_force_solution,
//                  ported in 4ac3df8e). With two pages the last-page rule and the
//                  single-page rule cannot be told apart.
//
// Whole notes, deliberately: the systems are what page breaking prices, and one glyph
// per measure keeps the snapshot a third of the size that quarter notes would make it.
//
// The bar numbers matter too. Every system except the very first one carries one above
// its staff, so staff-refpoint-extent is NOT the same on every system -- which is the
// asymmetry that made an earlier session read 11.528583 as a compression when the
// spacing was in fact uniform (HANDOFF 5.3: measure the vertical between staff
// refpoints, not between system origins). A fixture without that mix would be blind to
// a regression that went back to measuring from the wrong reference point.
title "Multi-page vertical spacing"

time 4/4

part melody

section Main {
  melody {
    c1 | d1 | e1 | f1 | g1 | f1 | e1 | d1 |
    c1 | d1 | e1 | f1 | g1 | f1 | e1 | d1 |
    c1 | d1 | e1 | f1 | g1 | f1 | e1 | d1 |
    c1 | d1 | e1 | f1 | g1 | f1 | e1 | d1 |
    c1 | d1 | e1 | f1 | g1 | f1 | e1 | d1 |
    c1 | d1 | e1 | f1 | g1 | f1 | e1 | d1 |
